{
  "teamId": 147,
  "year": 2018,
  "narrative": "The 2018 New York Yankees arrived with enormous expectations, having added Giancarlo Stanton to a roster already built around Aaron Judge, and they largely delivered, winning 100 games for the first time in nearly two decades. After a rocky opening week that included a brutal Opening Day grand slam allowed by David Robertson and a costly Aaron Judge double-play in Baltimore, the Yankees caught fire and didn't really cool off all spring. A 16-9 April gave way to a blistering 17-8 May powered by breakout performances from rookies Gleyber Torres and Miguel Andujar, who brought a jolt of youthful energy that the lineup hadn't anticipated needing quite so much.\n\nJune was the team's best month at 18-10, with the power-laden core finally clicking together. Stanton, Sanchez, Torres, and Judge all hit pivotal home runs within weeks of each other, and the Bronx felt like a powerhouse again. The acquisition of relievers Zack Britton and others at the deadline reflected front-office confidence, and while July and August were somewhat bumpier — bullpen meltdowns cost them dearly in both months, particularly in losses to Boston — the team never truly skidded. Neil Walker emerged as a quietly essential contributor, hitting clutch home runs down the stretch to help New York close out a 100-win season.\n\nThe October story, however, was a bittersweet coda. The Yankees dispatched Oakland in the Wild Card Game behind an Aaron Judge home run, then ran into the buzzsaw that was the Boston Red Sox in the ALDS. J.A. Happ was roughed up in Game 1, and though Judge and Gary Sanchez traded blows with Boston's lineup, the Yankees could not sustain enough offense or pitching across the series. A final-game defeat ended a season that had promised so much more, leaving a talented roster to wonder what might have been against a Red Sox team that simply would not be denied.\n\nStill, the 2018 Yankees represented a genuine resurgence. The 267 home runs set a franchise record at the time, the 3.78 ERA reflected a pitching staff that was good if not dominant, and the 100-win plateau validated the direction of the franchise. Torres and Andujar announced themselves as foundational pieces of the future, and the core of Judge, Stanton, and Sanchez remained one of the most fearsome in baseball. The postseason exit stung, but the foundation had been laid for something potentially greater.",
